<title>GERMANY: Gerling 95/96 group net profit falls.</title>
<headline>Gerling 95/96 group net profit falls.</headline>
<dateline>COLOGNE, Germany 1997-01-16</dateline>
<text>
<p>Business year to June 30 (in millions of marks unless otherwise stated)</p>
<p>    Group net profit			   110     vs 274</p>
<p>    Group premium income		     11,800  vs 11,000</p>
<p>    --domestic				   7,600   vs 7,400</p>
<p>    --foreign				    4,200   vs 3,600</p>
<p>    --primary insurance			8,200   vs 7,700</p>
<p>    --reinsurance				3,600   vs 3,300</p>
<p>    Life premium volume			192,900 vs 157,500</p>
<p>Underwriting earnings</p>
<p>    before allocations to reserves	213 loss vs 254 loss</p>
<p>    Allocation to reserves		   191     vs 110</p>
<p>    Group investments including trusts   35,700  vs 33,00</p>
<p>Investment earnings incl. interest</p>
<p>    on trusts				    2,400   vs 2,300</p>
<p>    Claims ratio (in pct)		    71.5    vs 71.0</p>
<p>    No. of staff				 9,762   vs 9,363</p>
<p>NOTE - Full name of the German insurer is Gerling Konzern Versicherungs Beteiligungs AG. The company said that the results did not include data from Frankona Rueckversicherungs AG which was sold in 1995. The net profit fall was due to the sale, which had inflated earnings from the previous period.</p>
<p>Discounting Frankona, profits would have risen to 106 million marks from 78 million marks, Gerling said.</p>
<p>Premium income rose chiefly because of growth in international business, whose share of Gerling's premium volume rose to 32.4 percent from 30.6 percent.</p>
<p>Foreign premium income rose 12.9 percent in the period while domestic premiums increased by 3.7 percent.</p>
<p>--Frankfurt Newsroom, +49 69 756525</p>
</text>
